  "ops.vehInTotal": "{vehicles} veh. in total",
  "ops.roadRoutingOrderAndKmByRoad": "{v0}, road routing {v1}/{total} (order and km by road) …",
  "ops.restStraightLine135": ", rest straight line × 1.35",
  "ops.roadRoutingOsrmOfRunsByRoad": "{v0}, road routing OSRM: {done} of {length} runs by road{v3}",
  "ops.method": "Method",
  "ops.demandDeploySpotsAhpRuns": "Demand, deploy spots (AHP), runs, limits",
  "ops.measuredDemandNetOutflow": "Measured demand: net outflow",
  "ops.perCell250MAndTimeOfDayBand": "Per cell ({CELL}) and time-of-day band the collector counts trip starts (available vehicle disappears or stable ID departs) and trip ends (vehicle appears or arrives) every 20 minutes across all operators (market); runs after a gap of more than 45 minutes do not count. <b>Net outflow</b> = (starts − k × ends) ÷ measured hours, k balances Σ starts and Σ ends of the municipality (see uncertainty and stability): positive means more vehicles leave the cell than arrive, it needs supply; negative means vehicles accumulate (a source for rebalancing). Standard error, range and lower bound are shown below each value. The operator gets its current share of the available fleet in the municipality. From 02/11/2026, origin–destination flows (flows/&lt;day&gt;.json, only operators with stable IDs, rows from 2 trips) additionally confirm outflow and inflow per cell: shown is for how many spots with net outflow the flows also show more departures than arrivals. Below {prelimH} measured hours, net outflow and stability are preliminary.",
  "ops.needFleetShortfall": "Need, fleet, shortfall",
  "ops.needMax0NetOutflowHHorizon": "<b>Need</b> = max(0, net outflow/h) × horizon (default {horizonH} h) + base stock; base stock = trip starts/h × {baseH} h, because starts and ends do not arrive at the same time. Rounded up, at least {minPerSpot}, at most {maxPerSpot} vehicles per spot. <b>Fleet</b> = the operator's available vehicles now within {spotRadiusM} m (the surroundings count, not just the coordinate). <b>Shortfall</b> = need − fleet. Example: 2.0 starts/h and 1.2 ends/h → net 0.8/h × 3 h + 2.0 = 4.4 → need 5; 2 vehicles nearby → shortfall 3.",
  "ops.deploySpotsMcda": "Deploy spots (MCDA)",
  "ops.factorsStandardisedTo0195th": "Factors standardised to 0–1 (95th percentile as upper bound, direction: more = better): <b>net outflow</b> as the cautious lower bound max(0, net − 2 × standard error) (inflow and uncertain outflow = 0), <b>demand</b> (starts/h), <b>public transport proximity</b> (stop points ≤ 200 m, 3 = full), <b>parking area</b> (e-scooter parking bay, official area or sharing hub ≤ 160 m = 1, bike rack ≤ 80 m = 0.5). Net outflow replaces the former “undersupply” (starts ÷ fleet), which was only derived. Whether demand and net outflow measure the same thing is shown by the rank correlation ρ across the candidate cells above the spot table (whole day, as of 01/11/2026: Stuttgart 0.13, Berlin 0.24, Duisburg 0.21 – the two factors measure different things, no double counting). The spot snaps to the area. No-parking zones from the operator's GBFS zones are an exclusion, not a weight; so are the city's official no-parking zones (Berlin: micromobility no-parking zones, Munich: geofences within 100 m of the parking areas), unless the spot lies on a designated parking area. Official parking areas (Berlin, Munich) count like e-scooter parking bays; source and licence are shown above the spot table and in the legend. In mandatory parking zones (GBFS <span class=\"mono\">station_parking</span>, e.g. Dott Leipzig) the spot snaps to the operator's nearest station (≤ 150 m); without a station the cell drops out. Minimum distance between spots.",
  "ops.weightsAhp": "Weights (AHP)",
  "ops.weight": "Weight",
  "ops.saatyScale1EquallyImportant3": "Saaty scale (1 = equally important, 3 = somewhat more important, 5 = clearly more important; row against column). Net outflow 2× demand: outflow is the direct reason to refill, demand ensures the spot is used. Weights = principal eigenvector, λ<sub>max</sub> = {lambdaMax}, consistency ratio CR = {cr} (&lt; 0.10; verified with numpy). If a factor is missing (e.g. OSM unreachable), it is left out and the remaining weights are renormalised. <b>Sensitivity:</b> each weight ±20 %, renormalised; “stable” = spot stays in the selection in all variants, “medium” ≥ 75 %, otherwise “uncertain”.",
  "ops.rebalanceSurplusMeans": "<b>Rebalance:</b> surplus means vehicles above the need of their cell, vehicles not moved for over 24 h and extra vehicles at spots; batteries below {minBatteryForMove} % are not moved but swapped. Surplus per cell follows the same need formula. <b>Priority by shortfall:</b> the spot with the largest shortfall gets the nearest surplus vehicles first (≤ {v1} km) and opens a run first; R1 covers the largest shortfall. Collect first, then drop off. <b>Battery swap:</b> only if charge is below {swapThreshold} % <b>and</b> range below {swapRangeKm} km (values in the form; if a feed reports only one of them, that one decides). Priority = 0.6 × energy gap + 0.4 × demand at the location, at most 50 batteries per run. <b>Workshop:</b> disabled vehicles, longest disabled first. <b>Stations:</b> refill stations at ≤ 20 % fill from stations at ≥ 80 % to 50 %.",
  "ops.bundlingByAreaOpenJob": "<b>Bundling by area:</b> the open job farthest from the depot opens a run (rebalancing: the job for the spot with the largest shortfall, on a tie the farthest), then the jobs nearest to the run's centre are added until capacity is reached (rebalancing: centre of pickup and destination). If the run takes longer than the set time (default {v0} h, driving + on site), it keeps the most nearby jobs that still fit; the distant ones go back and form their own runs. Anything beyond the number of runs per type is listed as “not scheduled” at the run list.",
  "ops.orderPerRunNearestNeighbour": "<b>Order per run:</b> nearest neighbour from six start points, then 2-opt and Or-opt on a distance matrix of the run; each move is evaluated over the changed edges (with asymmetric road costs using forward and backward sums), the result is the same as with the full run calculation. With “road routing” (default) the order is optimised by vehicle kilometres on the road (OSRM distance matrix), km and driving time come from the driven route (OSRM demo server, without traffic); the time limit applies to planning, by road a run may exceed it slightly (marked). Without road routing or on failure: straight line × 1.35 at {speedKmh} km/h. On-site times: 1.5 min pick up, 1 min drop off, 2.5 min battery, 3 min workshop.",
  "ops.limits": "Limits",
  "ops.idleTimeCountsFromFirst": "Idle time counts from the first sighting by the collector. Operators with rotating IDs produce more starts and destinations than real trips; for them, demand should be read relatively. Suppressed demand (nobody finds a vehicle) is not measured. The operator's deployments with new IDs count as trip ends: at its own deploy places, net outflow is therefore a lower bound. Parking areas and stop points from OpenStreetMap are as complete as the map. With a new collector snapshot, Operations recalculates with the new fleet as soon as it is shown.",
